 "prompt": "The repository at /testbed has a bug, described below. Fix it by changing the source code; do not modify or\nadd tests. When you are done, the project's existing test suite should pass.\n\n<issue>\nParser fails to handle WITH clauses containing JOURNAL, DATA, and NO DATA keywords\n\n#### Description\n\nThe SQL parser incorrectly processes WITH clauses that contain specific keywords like JOURNAL, DATA, and NO DATA. When parsing CREATE TABLE statements with these keywords in WITH clauses, the parser appears to get stuck in an infinite loop or fails to properly terminate parsing.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nimport sqlglot\n\n# This should parse successfully but causes issues\nsql = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE test_table (\n    id INT,\n    name VARCHAR(50)\n) WITH JOURNAL TABLE=journal_table\n\"\"\"\n\ntry:\n    parsed = sqlglot.parse(sql, dialect=\"teradata\")\n    print(\"Parsed successfully:\", parsed)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n\n# Similar issue with DATA keywords\nsql2 = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE test_table (\n    id INT,\n    name VARCHAR(50)\n) WITH DATA\n\"\"\"\n\ntry:\n    parsed = sqlglot.parse(sql2, dialect=\"teradata\")\n    print(\"Parsed successfully:\", parsed)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(\"Error:\", e)\n```\n\nThe parser seems to hang or fail when encountering these specific keyword combinations in WITH clauses, particularly affecting Teradata dialect parsing where these constructs are commonly used.\n</issue>\n",
